  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several device formats for organic electrochemical structures built around three key parts: a source, drain, and gate. Across the examples, the yellow and blue elements appear to represent conductive paths and contacts, showing how the same basic architecture can be adapted to very different mechanical and biological settings.\n\nAt the top, the comparison is between conventional rigid planar arrays and flexible, stretchable versions. The rigid layout uses straight patterned lines on a flat substrate, whereas the flexible design uses wavy interconnects that can bend and elongate without breaking, emphasizing mechanical compliance.\n\nThe center shows microfluidic integration, where the device is embedded beneath a transparent fluidic chamber with inlet and outlet ports. This suggests operation in liquid environments and controlled delivery of electrolyte over the active regions.\n\nThe bottom examples extend the concept into soft and three-dimensional forms. One device is incorporated into a porous 3D scaffold, implying intimate interaction with volumetric tissue-like materials. The other is a fibre-based configuration, where source, drain, and gate run along or across flexible strands and meet within a localized gel electrolyte region. Overall, the figure highlights the versatility of these devices for soft, biointerfaced electronics.",
